WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:07.660
A nucleus has no membranes inside it, yet it is not a uniform soup.

2
00:00:08.520 --> 00:00:13.580
Each chromosome keeps to its own territory, and open, active chromatin sorts apart

3
00:00:13.580 --> 00:00:15.480
from closed, quiet chromatin.

4
00:00:16.540 --> 00:00:19.600
Membraneless bodies gather machinery for particular jobs.

5
00:00:20.420 --> 00:00:25.880
The largest is the nucleolus, which makes ribosomal RNA and assembles ribosomal

6
00:00:25.880 --> 00:00:29.480
subunits. So what holds these things together without walls?

7
00:00:30.533 --> 00:00:32.633
Read the nucleolus from the inside out.

8
00:00:33.453 --> 00:00:35.993
The fibrillar centre holds the ribosomal genes.

9
00:00:37.013 --> 00:00:40.873
Around it, the dense fibrillar component begins to process the new RNA.

10
00:00:41.853 --> 00:00:46.353
The outer granular component is where subunits are assembled before export.

11
00:00:47.253 --> 00:00:51.973
In frog oocytes, the middle and outer layers behave as two liquids that coexist

12
00:00:51.973 --> 00:00:52.973
without mixing.

13
00:00:53.767 --> 00:00:56.007
One proposal is phase separation.

14
00:00:57.087 --> 00:01:01.567
Molecules with many weak binding sites demix from their surroundings, as oil

15
00:01:01.567 --> 00:01:02.567
separates from vinegar.

16
00:01:03.227 --> 00:01:05.427
But a bright spot can arise in other ways.

17
00:01:06.267 --> 00:01:11.287
A protein can bind clustered sites, or bridge chromatin so the fibre collapses into

18
00:01:11.287 --> 00:01:15.327
a globule. The three differ when you change the amount of protein.

19
00:01:16.300 --> 00:01:18.540
Phase separation predicts a threshold.

20
00:01:19.520 --> 00:01:22.420
Below a saturation concentration, no bodies form.

21
00:01:23.440 --> 00:01:27.740
Above it, extra protein goes into the bodies and they grow, while the concentration

22
00:01:27.740 --> 00:01:29.540
outside stays at the threshold.

23
00:01:30.460 --> 00:01:35.020
With made up data, bodies fill about two percent of the nucleus at two micromolar

24
00:01:35.020 --> 00:01:39.680
total, and about fourteen percent at eight, while the outside stays near one

25
00:01:39.680 --> 00:01:40.680
micromolar.

26
00:01:41.333 --> 00:01:42.653
So the test is simple.

27
00:01:43.533 --> 00:01:47.253
Change the amount of protein, and measure inside and outside the bodies.

28
00:01:48.073 --> 00:01:51.073
If the outside stays flat, that fits phase separation.

29
00:01:51.993 --> 00:01:57.073
If it rises in proportion, with a constant fraction left outside, that fits binding

30
00:01:57.073 --> 00:01:58.073
sites better.

31
00:01:58.817 --> 00:02:04.037
Round shapes, fusion, fluorescence recovery and hexanediol cannot tell these models

32
00:02:04.037 --> 00:02:09.397
apart. A protein that fails the test may still phase separate, as one part of a

33
00:02:09.397 --> 00:02:13.697
mixture. A protein that passes has not been shown to need its body.

34
00:02:14.577 --> 00:02:18.777
Keep the order: what is there, what it does, how it forms, and whether its form

35
00:02:18.777 --> 00:02:19.817
matters for its job.
